<item>
            <title>[LU-20841] LNet: acceptor listener teardown race</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20841</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;A client crash is seen in __wake_up_common(), called from the NET_RX_SOFTIRQ (napi_gro_receive -&amp;gt; tcp_v4_rcv -&amp;gt; tcp_child_process), right after LNet has been unloaded and reloaded. The waitqueue being woken is lnet_acceptor_waitq, reached from a listening socket whose sk_data_ready still points at lnet_acceptor_ready() in the module that is going away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several problems allow this:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;alternate&quot; type=&quot;square&quot;&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;sk_data_ready is installed and restored without holding sk-&amp;gt;sk_callback_lock, so a softirq can be using the old value&#160; while it is being replaced, and nothing waits for it to complete before sock_release() and the module unload. Install and restore the callback under write_lock_bh(&amp;amp;sk-&amp;gt;sk_callback_lock), let lnet_acceptor_ready() take the lock for read and NOOP if it lost the race, and call &#160;synchronize_net() before releasing the socket.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;ul class=&quot;alternate&quot; type=&quot;square&quot;&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The previous callback is saved in a single global, lnet_acceptor_state.pta_odata, overwritten by every add and never reset, hence stale across an unload/reload and NULL before the first listener is added. Store it per listening&#160;socket instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;ul class=&quot;alternate&quot; type=&quot;square&quot;&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;LNetNIFini() calls lnet_acceptor_stop() before lnet_shutdown_lndnets(). The acceptor thread exit path drains socket_list and releases the listeners itself, so when ksocknal_shutdown() later calls lnet_acceptor_remove_sockets() the listener is already gone and &quot;Interface %s not found&quot; is reported. The same inversion exists in the LNetNIInit() error path. Call lnet_acceptor_stop() after lnet_shutdown_lndnets(), as lnet_dyn_del_ni() and lnet_dyn_del_net() already do.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The four open coded refcount drops are folded into lnet_acceptor_put_socket(), socket_lock is taken with the _bh variants now that it is shared with a softirq callback, and the listening socket leaked when lnet_sock_listen() fails is freed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
